  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1059 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nWednesday was a tale of two areas across the Mid-South, with\nthose socked in with cloud coverage barely reaching into the 70s\nwhereas other locations under sunny skies reached the mid to\nupper 80s. Cloud coverage has remained across generally the\nnorthern half of the area into the overnight hours, however,\nlatest satellite trends do show some breaks in this coverage. The\nmain question for the remainder of the overnight hours into\nThursday will be if some of the cloud coverage to our northeast\nwill be able to filter back into the Mid-South. If this does\nhappen, high temperatures will likely be below normal for much of\nthe area in the upper 70s to low 80s. However, if this cloud\ncoverage does not rebuild through the region, temperatures would\nbe closer to normal in the mid 80s.\n\nBy Friday, an upper-level ridge will move over the region,\nallowing temperatures to slowly return to above-normal. High\ntemperatures are forecast to be generally in the mid 80s across\nthe Mid-South through the weekend and into early next week. The\naforementioned ridge will begin to break down by Sunday, allowing\nfor a few week disturbances to pass through. While confidence is\nlow, these disturbances will allow for some isolated shower and\nthunderstorm chances to return Sunday into the early portion of\nnext week. Unfortunately, this will still likely not bring\nwidespread, meaningful precipitation to most of the area.\n\nInto the middle of next week and the end of the current forecast\nperiod, things do become quite uncertain. While guidance does\nagree that an upper-level trough and corresponding low pressure\nsystem will move off the Rocky Mountains, the timing and strength\nof this system is highly variable. In addition, guidance is\nstruggling to resolve the interaction of this system with\nHurricane Polo in the Pacific Ocean, which could play a role in\nhow this trough axis progresses. Ensemble guidance is quite\nspread Wednesday through the end of next week. ENS guidance\nfavors a sharper cool down that remains postfrontal, while GEFS\nguidance has a more shallow cool down with temperatures quickly\nrebounding after. It does appear that we will get our first shot\nof some meaningful precipitation, but the timing is still\ncomplex. Additional confidence will hopefully be gained in future\nforecasts as more guidance comes in.",
